# `pole` (a post or rod) is often mistakenly used instead of `poll` (a survey or voting).
expr modifier [exit, opinion, public, recent, latest, national, presidential, straw, online, statewide]
expr verb [conduct, conducts, conducted, conducting, organize, organizes, organized, organizing, take, takes, took, taking]
expr main [<(@modifier pole), pole>, <(@verb [a, an, the] pole), pole>, <([run, runs, running, ran] [a, an, the] pole), pole>]

let message "Did you mean `poll`? A `poll` is a survey or vote; a `pole` is a long rod or post."
let description "Looks for mixing up the noun `poll` with `pole`."
let kind "WordChoice"
let becomes "poll"

test "An opinion pole was conducted before the election." "An opinion poll was conducted before the election."
test "The exit pole showed a close race." "The exit poll showed a close race."
test "We should conduct a pole to gauge user feedback." "We should conduct a poll to gauge user feedback."
test "The newspaper took a pole of local residents." "The newspaper took a poll of local residents."
test "A recent pole revealed shifting voter preferences." "A recent poll revealed shifting voter preferences."
test "The latest pole has the incumbent ahead." "The latest poll has the incumbent ahead."
test "They organized a straw pole at the conference." "They organized a straw poll at the conference."
test "A national pole indicates widespread support." "A national poll indicates widespread support."
test "Participate in our online pole." "Participate in our online poll."

allows "He leaned against the telephone pole."
allows "The flag was raised to the top of the pole."
allows "We reached the North Pole after three weeks."
allows "The pole vault competition starts tomorrow."
